# server: Execute system APCs outside signal handlers.
# https://gitlab.winehq.org/wine/wine/-/merge_requests/12645 state=opened
# System APCs can call functions such as malloc(), free(), and
pthread_create(). Executing them from SIGUSR1 can re-enter an interrupted
library operation, causing deadlocks or allocator failures.

Prefer a dedicated native worker, with application threads already
waiting in the server as a fallback. Introduce SELECT_SYSTEM and
STATUS_SYSTEM_APC to distinguish these operations from asynchronous I/O
APCs, which retain signal delivery to preserve their completion timing.

Redistribute undelivered APCs when their executor exits. Once APC shutdown
starts, reject further system and I/O APCs before cleaning up the queues.
This prevents cleanup from requeueing work after the final queue drain
and leaving callers waiting indefinitely.

Keep the worker available until the last application thread exits,
preserving the process exit code and debug event. Terminate the process
if its worker disappears unexpectedly, and handle teardown that removes
the thread issuing the current request.

Also extend cancellation of eligible pending I/O to all thread-death
paths, including pipe disconnections, rather than only explicit thread
termination requests. Complete I/O APCs destroyed without a result with
STATUS_CANCELLED instead of propagating their notification status.
